# TODO - Implement and integrate the AirDrop Wi-Fi/BLE path

## Owner directive

AirDrop is a required project outcome alongside native Wi-Fi and Bluetooth.
It uses BLE discovery and Apple's peer-to-peer Wi-Fi technology, rather than
being a file transfer implemented solely by the Bluetooth radio. The combo
chip must be integrated with the required native macOS protocols.

## Acceptance

- [ ] Research the exact Tahoe native AWDL/interface/packet/event contracts
      and independently implement the required control and data paths.
- [ ] Implement peer discovery, action-frame handling, timing and permitted
      off-channel scheduling appropriate to the single-chain 2.4 GHz AR9565.
      Do not advertise unavailable 5 GHz or unsupported radio capabilities.
- [ ] Integrate the Bluetooth BLE and native macOS AirDrop/Continuity paths,
      including IPv6/multicast discovery and packet ownership where required.
- [ ] Preserve normal associated Wi-Fi, Bluetooth coexistence, channel/power
      policy, key lifetimes and suspend/recovery during peer-to-peer work.
- [ ] Use source-backed offline packet/state fixtures; document actual
      implemented capabilities and remaining assumptions without synthesizing
      AirDrop success from an awdl0 name, a registry property or DHCP alone.

All source acceptance above is independent of physical device tests. Actual
nearby discovery and bidirectional transfer belong to the single final
owner-controlled acceptance TODO, which remains open until the owner closes it.

