## Source progress — 2026-11-10: bounded AWDL action-frame ingress

`src/wifi/AwdlAction.{hpp,cpp}` is compiled into the **ordinary** Xcode
production source target. It parses the documented 802.11 vendor-specific
AWDL action format (Apple BSSID and OUI, type 8, version 1.0, PSF/MIF),
reads both little-endian 32-bit transmit timestamps, and bounds each
16-bit-length TLV before publishing a header/count snapshot. It permits
only the documented one- or two-octet zero padding and rejects malformed
lengths, fragments, wrong vendor/version, unauthorized protected frames and
aliased output. Individual TLV payload views remain borrowed until the RX
owner releases the frame. Unknown TLV values are deliberately opaque.

Evidence: the independent AWDL layout described by Stute et al.,
https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.03156, and Project Zero's AWDL protocol
analysis:
https://projectzero.google/2020/12/an-ios-zero-click-radio-proximity.html.
`tests/test_awdl_action.cpp` exercises synthetic PSF/MIF, negative wire
syntax, aliasing and short/trailing data. Focused C++14/C++17 ASan+UBSan,
strict Clang-Tidy on both new translation units, and formatting passed.
The latest full `tools/validate.py --tidy --tests --json` reported **582
checks, 548 passes, 34 previously known Tidy failures, zero skips** and no
new failing files. Existing strict Tidy violations remain unresolved.

**Not integrated yet:** physical RF RX provenance, native AWDL interface,
peer election/timing, channel dwell, regulatory scheduling, BLE service
policy, AirDrop/Continuity host linkage and actual IPv6 transfer. Merely
compiling this fail-closed syntax parser does not imply any macOS AWDL or
Bluetooth functionality. This P0 TODO remains open.
